  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1114 PM CST SUN NOV 1 2026\n\nOnly an isolated light shower may be noted across the far NRN\ncounties over the next few hours across Central Alabama. The\nupper trough and associated shortwave activity is pushing EWD and\naway from the area now extending from the Great Lakes to Georgia.\nThis wave will continue EWD and away from the region Monday\nmorning with a break in the rain activity for Monday into much of\nthe day Tuesday ahead of the next surface frontal system.\n\nBy Tuesday, our next system approaches as a low moves out of ERN\nKansas across to Illinois and Michigan by Thursday. As it does\nso, it will drag a front across much of the Gulf Coast States\nincluding Central Alabama. We could see a few more thunderstorms\nwith enough forcing from the front, along with some heavy rain at\ntimes. Latest guidance looks to weaken storms as they get closer\nTuesday night with some of the flow potentially slow at times\nwith the lower stream system. This is pointing more toward heavy\nrain potential for Tuesday night into Wednesday than severe, more\nso in the NW half of Central Alabama. Behind it for Thursday,\nthere will be a small chance for some wrap-around showers behind\nthe front as the upper trough pushes across Eastern Conus. Then\non Friday a second (rain-free) front is expected to bring down a\nmuch colder Canadian air mass to lower the temperatures back into\nthe 40s (and maybe a few upper 30s north) for lows for Central\nAlabama for next weekend.\n\n08",
